WEBVTT

00:00.670 --> 00:04.045
This triangle that we have right
over here is a right triangle.

00:04.045 --> 00:06.600
And it's a right triangle
because it has a 90 degree

00:06.600 --> 00:09.240
angle, or has a
right angle in it.

00:09.240 --> 00:12.520
Now, we call the longest
side of a right triangle,

00:12.520 --> 00:14.599
we call that side,
and you could either

00:14.599 --> 00:17.140
view it as the longest side of
the right triangle or the side

00:17.140 --> 00:20.980
opposite the 90 degree angle,
it is called a hypotenuse.

00:20.980 --> 00:23.740
It's a very fancy word
for a fairly simple idea,

00:23.740 --> 00:26.140
just the longest side of a
right triangle or the side

00:26.140 --> 00:27.542
opposite the 90 degree angle.

00:27.542 --> 00:29.500
And it's just good to
know that because someone

00:29.500 --> 00:30.090
might say hypotenuse.

00:30.090 --> 00:32.548
You're like, oh, they're just
talking about this side right

00:32.548 --> 00:36.580
here, the side longest, the side
opposite the 90 degree angle.

00:36.580 --> 00:38.860
Now, what I want
to do in this video

00:38.860 --> 00:42.167
is prove a relationship, a
very famous relationship.

00:42.167 --> 00:43.750
And you might see
where this is going.

00:43.750 --> 00:46.370
A very famous relationship
between the lengths

00:46.370 --> 00:48.840
of the sides of
a right triangle.

00:48.840 --> 00:53.210
So let's say that the length of
AC, so uppercase A, uppercase

00:53.210 --> 00:55.930
C, let's call that
length lowercase a.

00:55.930 --> 01:00.040
Let's call the length of BC
lowercase b right over here.

01:00.040 --> 01:03.420
I'll use uppercases for
points, lowercases for lengths.

01:03.420 --> 01:06.630
And let's call the length of the
hypotenuse, the length of AB,

01:06.630 --> 01:07.822
let's call that c.

01:07.822 --> 01:10.030
And let's see if we can come
up with the relationship

01:10.030 --> 01:12.790
between a, b, and c.

01:12.790 --> 01:14.780
And to do that I'm
first going to construct

01:14.780 --> 01:16.410
another line or
another segment, I

01:16.410 --> 01:19.520
should say, between
c and the hypotenuse.

01:19.520 --> 01:21.600
And I'm going to
construct it so that they

01:21.600 --> 01:23.880
intersect at a right angle.

01:23.880 --> 01:25.006
And you can always do that.

01:25.006 --> 01:27.005
And we'll call this point
right over here we'll.

01:27.005 --> 01:28.120
Call this point capital D.

01:28.120 --> 01:31.010
And if you're wondering,
how can you always do that?

01:31.010 --> 01:33.634
You could imagine rotating
this entire triangle like this.

01:33.634 --> 01:36.050
This isn't a rigorous proof,
but it just kind of gives you

01:36.050 --> 01:38.100
the general idea of
how you can always

01:38.100 --> 01:39.810
construct a point like this.

01:39.810 --> 01:41.260
So if I've rotated it around.

01:41.260 --> 01:44.750
So now our hypotenuse, we're
now sitting on our hypotenuse.

01:44.750 --> 01:48.414
This is now point
B, this is point A.

01:48.414 --> 01:50.580
So we've rotated the whole
thing all the way around.

01:50.580 --> 01:52.710
This is point C. You
could imagine just

01:52.710 --> 01:55.820
dropping a rock from point C,
maybe with a string attached,

01:55.820 --> 01:59.460
and it would hit the
hypotenuse at a right angle.

01:59.460 --> 02:02.980
So that's all we did here to
establish segment CD into where

02:02.980 --> 02:05.570
we put our point D
right over there.

02:05.570 --> 02:07.220
And the reason why
did that is now we

02:07.220 --> 02:09.289
can do all sorts of
interesting relationships

02:09.289 --> 02:10.490
between similar triangles.

02:10.490 --> 02:12.180
Because we have
three triangles here.

02:12.180 --> 02:15.604
We have triangle ADC,
we have triangle DBC,

02:15.604 --> 02:17.520
and then we have the
larger original triangle.

02:17.520 --> 02:19.890
And we can hopefully
establish similarity

02:19.890 --> 02:21.980
between those triangles.

02:21.980 --> 02:27.590
And first I'll show you that ADC
is similar to the larger one.

02:27.590 --> 02:29.710
Because both of them
have a right angle.

02:29.710 --> 02:32.070
ADC has a right angle
right over here.

02:32.070 --> 02:33.571
Clearly if this
angle is 90 degrees,

02:33.571 --> 02:35.653
then this angle is going
to be 90 degrees as well.

02:35.653 --> 02:36.660
They are supplementary.

02:36.660 --> 02:38.510
They have to add up to 180.

02:38.510 --> 02:40.440
And so they both have
a right angle in them.

02:40.440 --> 02:42.060
So the smaller one
has a right angle.

02:42.060 --> 02:43.590
The larger one clearly
has a right angle.

02:43.590 --> 02:44.840
That's where we started from.

02:44.840 --> 02:48.690
And they also both
share this angle right

02:48.690 --> 02:52.150
over here, angle
DAC or BAC, however

02:52.150 --> 02:53.580
you want to refer to it.

02:53.580 --> 02:56.720
So we can actually write
down that triangle.

02:56.720 --> 03:00.290
I'm going to start with
the smaller one, ADC.

03:00.290 --> 03:02.190
And maybe I'll shade
it in right over here.

03:02.190 --> 03:04.023
So this is the triangle
we're talking about.

03:04.023 --> 03:05.429
Triangle ADC.

03:05.429 --> 03:07.470
And I went from the blue
angle to the right angle

03:07.470 --> 03:10.620
to the unlabeled angle from the
point of view of triangle ADC.

03:10.620 --> 03:13.860
This right angle isn't applying
to that right over there.

03:13.860 --> 03:15.820
It's applying to
the larger triangle.

03:15.820 --> 03:24.820
So we could say triangle
ADC is similar to triangle--

03:24.820 --> 03:27.130
once again, you want to
start at the blue angle.

03:27.130 --> 03:29.500
A. Then we went to
the right angle.

03:29.500 --> 03:32.220
So we have to go to
the right angle again.

03:32.220 --> 03:32.830
So it's ACB.

03:37.190 --> 03:39.270
And because they're
similar, we can set up

03:39.270 --> 03:42.220
a relationship between
the ratios of their sides.

03:42.220 --> 03:44.705
For example, we know the
ratio of corresponding sides

03:44.705 --> 03:47.080
are going to do, well, in
general for a similar triangle,

03:47.080 --> 03:48.640
we know the ratio of
the corresponding sides

03:48.640 --> 03:49.890
are going to be a constant.

03:49.890 --> 03:54.100
So we could take the ratio of
the hypotenuse of the smaller

03:54.100 --> 03:54.960
triangle.

03:54.960 --> 03:57.350
So the hypotenuse is AC.

03:57.350 --> 04:00.710
So AC over the hypotenuse
over the larger one, which

04:00.710 --> 04:10.480
is a AB, AC over AB is going
to be the same thing as AD

04:10.480 --> 04:14.180
as one of the legs, AD.

04:14.180 --> 04:16.959
And just to show that, I'm just
taking corresponding points

04:16.959 --> 04:23.794
on both similar triangles,
this is AD over AC.

04:23.794 --> 04:25.960
You could look at these
triangles yourself and show,

04:25.960 --> 04:29.930
look, AD, point AD, is
between the blue angle

04:29.930 --> 04:31.410
and the right angle.

04:31.410 --> 04:34.760
Sorry, side AD is between the
blue angle and the right angle.

04:34.760 --> 04:38.025
Side AC is between the blue
angle and the right angle

04:38.025 --> 04:39.010
on the larger triangle.

04:39.010 --> 04:40.950
So both of these are
from the larger triangle.

04:40.950 --> 04:43.660
These are the corresponding
sides on the smaller triangle.

04:43.660 --> 04:46.990
And if that is confusing
looking at them visually,

04:46.990 --> 04:50.199
as long as we wrote our
similarity statement correctly,

04:50.199 --> 04:51.990
you can just find the
corresponding points.

04:51.990 --> 04:56.590
AC corresponds to AB
on the larger triangle,

04:56.590 --> 04:58.840
AD on the smaller
triangle corresponds

04:58.840 --> 05:02.330
to AC on the larger triangle.

05:02.330 --> 05:06.920
And we know that AC, we can
rewrite that as lowercase a.

05:06.920 --> 05:10.860
AC is lowercase a.

05:10.860 --> 05:16.810
We don't have any
label for AD or for AB.

05:16.810 --> 05:18.900
Sorry, we do have
a label for AB.

05:18.900 --> 05:20.590
That is c right over here.

05:20.590 --> 05:23.790
We don't have a label for AD.

05:23.790 --> 05:26.840
So AD, let's just
call that lowercase d.

05:26.840 --> 05:30.400
So lowercase d applies to
that part right over there.

05:30.400 --> 05:33.560
c applies to that entire
part right over there.

05:33.560 --> 05:35.905
And then we'll call DB,
let's call that length e.

05:35.905 --> 05:38.700
That'll just make things a
little bit simpler for us.

05:38.700 --> 05:41.760
So AD we'll just call d.

05:41.760 --> 05:43.850
And so we have a over
c is equal to d over a.

05:43.850 --> 05:47.830
If we cross multiply, you have
a times a, which is a squared,

05:47.830 --> 05:50.791
is equal to c times
d, which is cd.

05:50.791 --> 05:52.790
So that's a little bit
of an interesting result.

05:52.790 --> 05:54.789
Let's see what we can do
with the other triangle

05:54.789 --> 05:55.930
right over here.

05:55.930 --> 05:57.940
So this triangle
right over here.

05:57.940 --> 05:59.490
So once again, it
has a right angle.

05:59.490 --> 06:00.865
The larger one
has a right angle.

06:00.865 --> 06:04.270
And they both share this
angle right over here.

06:04.270 --> 06:07.070
So by angle, angle
similarity, the two triangles

06:07.070 --> 06:08.210
are going to be similar.

06:08.210 --> 06:11.040
So we could say triangle
BDC, we went from pink

06:11.040 --> 06:12.970
to right to not labeled.

06:12.970 --> 06:20.352
So triangle BDC is
similar to triangle.

06:20.352 --> 06:22.310
Now we're going to look
at the larger triangle,

06:22.310 --> 06:23.430
we're going to start
at the pink angle.

06:23.430 --> 06:25.567
B. Now we're going to
go to the right angle.

06:25.567 --> 06:26.066
CA.

06:29.190 --> 06:31.680
BCA.

06:31.680 --> 06:34.979
From pink angle to right
angle to non-labeled angle,

06:34.979 --> 06:36.520
at least from the
point of view here.

06:36.520 --> 06:38.420
We labeled it before
with that blue.

06:38.420 --> 06:40.620
So now let's set up some
type of relationship here.

06:40.620 --> 06:45.040
We can say that the ratio on
the smaller triangle, BC, side

06:45.040 --> 06:50.130
BC over BA, BC over
BA, once again,

06:50.130 --> 06:53.230
we're taking the
hypotenuses of both of them.

06:53.230 --> 07:00.593
So BC over BA is going
to be equal to BD.

07:00.593 --> 07:02.590
Let me do this in another color.

07:02.590 --> 07:03.450
BD.

07:03.450 --> 07:04.890
So this is one of the legs.

07:04.890 --> 07:05.570
BD.

07:05.570 --> 07:07.430
The way I drew it
is the shorter legs.

07:07.430 --> 07:10.370
BD over BC.

07:10.370 --> 07:12.770
I'm just taking the
corresponding vertices.

07:12.770 --> 07:14.600
Over BC.

07:14.600 --> 07:18.203
And once again, we know BC is
the same thing as lowercase b.

07:18.203 --> 07:20.322
BC is lowercase b.

07:20.322 --> 07:22.926
BA is lowercase c.

07:25.570 --> 07:29.740
And then BD we defined
as lowercase e.

07:29.740 --> 07:31.260
So this is lowercase e.

07:31.260 --> 07:33.210
We can cross
multiply here and we

07:33.210 --> 07:37.830
get b times b, which, and I've
mentioned this in many videos,

07:37.830 --> 07:40.310
cross multiplying is really
the same thing as multiplying

07:40.310 --> 07:42.680
both sides by both denominators.

07:42.680 --> 07:47.960
b times b is b squared
is equal to ce.

07:47.960 --> 07:50.010
And now we can do something
kind of interesting.

07:50.010 --> 07:51.406
We can add these two statements.

07:51.406 --> 07:53.030
Let me rewrite the
statement down here.

07:53.030 --> 07:56.100
So b squared is equal to ce.

07:56.100 --> 07:58.310
So if we add the
left hand sides,

07:58.310 --> 08:02.120
we get a squared plus b squared.

08:02.120 --> 08:09.420
a squared plus b squared
is equal to cd plus ce.

08:12.595 --> 08:14.917
And then we have a c
both of these terms,

08:14.917 --> 08:16.000
so we could factor it out.

08:16.000 --> 08:19.880
So this is going to be equal
to-- we can factor out the c.

08:19.880 --> 08:22.952
It's going to be equal
to c times d plus e.

08:22.952 --> 08:29.790
c times d plus e and
close the parentheses.

08:29.790 --> 08:31.460
Now what is d plus e?

08:31.460 --> 08:34.159
d is this length,
e is this length.

08:34.159 --> 08:37.169
So d plus e is actually
going to be c as well.

08:37.169 --> 08:38.496
So this is going to be c.

08:38.496 --> 08:41.039
So you have c times c,
which is just the same thing

08:41.039 --> 08:43.030
as c squared.

08:43.030 --> 08:45.700
So now we have an
interesting relationship.

08:45.700 --> 08:51.150
We have that a squared plus b
squared is equal to c squared.

08:51.150 --> 08:52.580
Let me rewrite that.

08:52.580 --> 08:54.300
a squared.

08:54.300 --> 08:58.623
Well, let me just do
an arbitrary new color.

08:58.623 --> 09:02.380
I deleted that by accident,
so let me rewrite it.

09:02.380 --> 09:07.390
So we've just established
that a squared plus b squared

09:07.390 --> 09:09.400
is equal to c squared.

09:09.400 --> 09:11.320
And this is just an
arbitrary right triangle.

09:11.320 --> 09:13.590
This is true for any
two right triangles.

09:13.590 --> 09:17.120
We've just established that
the sum of the squares of each

09:17.120 --> 09:20.060
of the legs is equal to the
square of the hypotenuse.

09:20.060 --> 09:22.550
And this is probably
what's easily

09:22.550 --> 09:26.220
one of the most famous
theorem in mathematics, named

09:26.220 --> 09:27.360
for Pythagoras.

09:27.360 --> 09:30.370
Not clear if he's the first
person to establish this,

09:30.370 --> 09:32.310
but it's called the
Pythagorean Theorem.

09:38.290 --> 09:41.469
And it's really the basis of,
well, all not all of geometry,

09:41.469 --> 09:43.510
but a lot of the geometry
that we're going to do.

09:43.510 --> 09:45.880
And it forms the basis of a
lot of the trigonometry we're

09:45.880 --> 09:46.230
going to do.

09:46.230 --> 09:47.550
And it's a really
useful way, if you

09:47.550 --> 09:49.299
know two of the sides
of a right triangle,

09:49.299 --> 09:51.890
you can always find the third.
